Date: Mon, 4 Jan 88 13:37:53 PST
From: coherent!dplatt@ames.arpa (Dave Platt)
Subject: Suggestion re the new Kermit
Keywords: MacKermit 0.9(36)

There's one change that could be made to the Macintosh version of Kermit
that would be of great help: minimal support, at least, for the Edit menu.

Currently, MacKermit doesn't permit any cutting and pasting at all, either
within Kermit windows or within desk-accessory windows.  It would be nice
(and quite convenient) to be able to copy text from a Kermit window (lots
easier than saving stuff to the session log), and to paste text from the
clipboard into a Kermit window (thus transmitting the text as if it had been
typed by hand).  I realize that the internal design of CKMKER's windows
might make text-copying difficult to implement... is it possible?

Even if copying and pasting within Kermit windows is prohibitively
difficult, I really feel that MacKermit should support the usual Edit menu
capabilities when a desk accessory window is in front.  It's currently
almost impossible to use a simple text-editing accessory such as SigmaEdit
or MockWrite, because MacKermit doesn't provide any way to copy, cut, or
paste.  I seem to recall that this flies in the face of one of Apple's
user-interface guidelines: if you support desk accessories, you must supply
the Edit menu and have the usual Edit capabilities in their standard
positions.

It appears that it wouldn't be very difficult to include the Edit menu in
the MacKermit menu bar, and simply disable the Edit menu (or all of the
commands within the menu) when the frontmost window on the screen belongs to
MacKermit.  Any chance that this could be implemented for the release (1.0)
version of MacKermit?

P.S... many thanks for the enhancements to b4, Paul!

------------------------------

Date: Mon, 4 Jan 88 22:40 CST
From: Les LaCroix <LLACROIX%carleton.edu@RELAY.CS.NET>
Subject: MacKermit 0.9(36)B4, ...B3
Keywords: MacKermit 0.9(36)

Last Saturday (Jan 2) I tried dialing up the various Kermit distribution
servers to get a copy of MacKermit0.9(36)B4 (as stored in xkm936.hqx).
Okstate didn't have it, but the University of Toledo did.  Unfortunately,
when I got it back it said it was ...B3, not B4.  So, I have a question and
a few comments:

Will ...B4 be available for downloading via a Kermit server?  I cannot do
FTPs from here (anonymous or otherwise).

Comment 1:  good job!

Comment 2: I found a kink or two in the "Set key macros..." section: I could
find no way to define the two-character sequence of ESC (ASCII 27) followed
by any ASCII digit, say '0'.  I stumbled across this curiosity by misreading
the "ESC O w" as "ESC 0 w" in my Vis100 manual.  Anyway, I tried "\0270w",
which was later displayed as "\14w" (270-256=14).  I don't know if "ESC 0"
(or 1, etc) would ever happen in nature, but I suppose it would be nice to
do just in case.  How about stopping the numeric interpretation after 3
digits, since it appears that the arithmetic is modulo 256 anyway?

Comment 3: I also tried "\27\0w", which was later displayed as "\27".  I can
only guess that the key macros are stored as standard C strings (terminated
with '\0'), and therefore cannot contain ASCII NULs.  Again, I don't know if
there would ever be a need to include a NUL in a key macro.

Les LaCroix		llacroix@carleton.edu   (csnet)
SPSS, Inc.		llacroix@carleton.csnet (bitnet)
402 Washington, Northfield MN USA 55057-2027

