Subject: re: MacKermit Messages
Reply-To: paul@tut.cis.ohio-state.edu

Thanks everyone for all the comments.  To save a bit of time, I am
replying to all of the message that Christine sent me...

Yes, MacKermit does _still_ need to be made to work on the modem port.
If anyone wants to make the changes, I will add it in (It isn't that
hard, but I havn't had time to do it yet).

I have fixed (at least partially) the "zchki failed" loop.  It now
does 3 different (cryptic) messages, but doesn't loop.

I will look into the Command-R problem, and the 1024k retry loop.
If you set the send packet size to I, but the sending side says it
will use J characters, and J < I, then Kermit will use J (to be safe:
slow transfer is better than none at all).

I have fixed the even parity bug.  MacKermit is now using the C-Kermit
software parity stuff again (for the same reasons as it did a while
ago: the Mac won't do mark parity in the driver, and sometimes won't
do even and odd right).

Yes, there is an EDIT menu, that does work with DA editors.

A set default volume dialog would be real nice (hint, hint).  I won't
have time to do it for a while, but if someone else wants to...

The kermit I am running (0.9(37) on an XL, system 3.2) seems to do
insert character just fine.  Any pointers on why it may overstrike?

The keyboard stuff still needs work.  I canget 8 bits through now, but
the Option key handling is still not right, and Meta may not be
either.

The terminal emulator needs a good hard look.  No atributes are saved,
and it doesn't seem to be redrawn after the transfer status dialog
goes away.  I don't know exactly what all to do about this, but
eventually I would like to see an emulator window that has scrollback,
will do cut/paste (paste "types" the characters at the serial port),
and do refresh correctly...

Kermit drops DTR because it actually closes the serial driver on exit
(thus ending a long-standing bug).  I will see about making it stay up
on close (maybe as yet another option).

I also fixed the bug with \break in the set macro dialog.  It now puts
the right stuff back into the edit line all the time.

I will change the code so that \nnnnn macros stop after 3 digits.
The macro strings are now Pascal strings, however (so you can include
\0 as a character).

		-- Paul
